1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:04,068
The brush uncovers a staff and a bundle of written sheets.

2
00:00:04,068 --> 00:00:07,966
Everything else in this gallery was made by people who lived here.

3
00:00:10,933 --> 00:00:13,108
This was made by somebody passing through,

4
00:00:13,108 --> 00:00:20,186
who wrote down what struck him as strange — and what strikes an outsider as strange is often what an insider never thought to record.

5
00:00:24,100 --> 00:00:26,682
The accounts themselves, in varying states.

6
00:00:26,682 --> 00:00:31,142
Some survive whole; one survives only in fragments quoted by other writers.

7
00:00:31,142 --> 00:00:34,429
Indian texts and inscriptions, which let us check them.

8
00:00:34,429 --> 00:00:39,306
Reading a traveller against the ground is the method throughout this episode.

9
00:00:42,733 --> 00:00:43,909
Five, in order.

10
00:00:43,909 --> 00:00:49,051
Megasthenes, around 300 BCE, a Greek ambassador at the Mauryan court.

11
00:00:49,051 --> 00:00:54,633
Fa-Hien, early fifth century CE, a Chinese Buddhist monk looking for texts.

12
00:00:54,633 --> 00:01:02,059
Hiuen Tsang, seventh century, another Chinese Buddhist, who stayed years and studied at Nalanda.

13
00:01:02,933 --> 00:01:07,711
I-Tsing, shortly after, who travelled by sea and wrote about monastic practice.

14
00:01:07,711 --> 00:01:15,655
And Al-Biruni, eleventh century, a Central Asian scholar who came with Mahmud of Ghazni's campaigns and wrote a study of India.

15
00:01:17,767 --> 00:01:24,313
Megasthenes wrote the Indica, and his description of Pataliputra's timber palisade is confirmed by excavation.

16
00:01:24,313 --> 00:01:30,329
But the Indica does not survive: we have it only as passages quoted by later Greek and Roman writers,

17
00:01:30,329 --> 00:01:32,693
who selected what interested them.

18
00:01:34,600 --> 00:01:39,345
His famous division of Indian society into seven classes does not match any Indian source,

19
00:01:39,345 --> 00:01:44,024
and is usually read as an outsider trying to fit what he saw into a familiar frame.

20
00:01:49,933 --> 00:01:54,691
The Buddhist pilgrims came for texts and teachers, and that shapes what they noticed.

21
00:01:54,691 --> 00:01:57,844
Fa-Hien records the Ganga plains as safe and prosperous,

22
00:01:57,844 --> 00:02:03,123
with free hospitals and rest houses — and also the exclusion of chandalas from the towns.

23
00:02:06,433 --> 00:02:11,969
Hiuen Tsang gives us Harsha's reign, Nalanda from the inside, and a great deal of geography.

24
00:02:11,969 --> 00:02:16,492
I-Tsing, travelling by sea, describes monastic rules and the voyage itself.

25
00:02:16,492 --> 00:02:20,780
All three are devout men describing a country they came to revere.

26
00:02:25,233 --> 00:02:29,010
Al-Biruni is different and worth more attention than he usually gets.

27
00:02:29,010 --> 00:02:37,158
He learned Sanskrit in order to read Indian texts directly, studied Indian astronomy and mathematics seriously enough to compare them with Greek work,

28
00:02:37,158 --> 00:02:40,965
and wrote his Kitab-ul-Hind as a study rather than a travelogue.

29
00:02:46,300 --> 00:02:50,796
He is also candid about the difficulty: he writes about the barriers of language,

30
00:02:50,796 --> 00:02:55,019
and about the mutual suspicion created by the campaigns he had arrived with.

31
00:02:55,019 --> 00:02:58,992
An honest source will tell you about its own limits, and his does.

32
00:03:02,233 --> 00:03:03,981
How far can you trust them?

33
00:03:03,981 --> 00:03:05,666
Both positions have force.

34
00:03:05,666 --> 00:03:11,844
The outsider records what the insider takes for granted — roads, food, prices, the look of a city.

35
00:03:11,844 --> 00:03:16,587
But each of these men came for a reason: an ambassador reporting to a king,

36
00:03:16,587 --> 00:03:21,255
pilgrims seeking a holy land, a scholar attached to an invading army.

37
00:03:22,133 --> 00:03:23,309
Each saw through that.

38
00:03:23,309 --> 00:03:25,712
The method is never to take a traveller alone:

39
00:03:25,712 --> 00:03:30,767
check him against inscriptions and the ground, and say in your answer what he was there for.

40
00:03:46,900 --> 00:03:50,308
Here is the memory tip, carved in stone.

41
00:03:50,308 --> 00:03:57,424
Five travellers, in order: Megasthenes, Fa-Hien, Hiuen Tsang, I-Tsing, Al-Biruni.

42
00:04:00,467 --> 00:04:01,176
Dig Quiz.

43
00:04:01,176 --> 00:04:02,524
Pause and name it.

44
00:04:02,524 --> 00:04:08,853
First: the account of India that survives only as fragments quoted by later writers.

45
00:04:10,733 --> 00:04:12,627
The Indica of Megasthenes.

46
00:04:12,627 --> 00:04:18,517
Second: the Chinese pilgrim who travelled by sea and wrote about monastic practice.

47
00:04:18,517 --> 00:04:19,449
I-Tsing.

48
00:04:20,633 --> 00:04:25,077
Third: the scholar who learned Sanskrit and wrote the Kitab-ul-Hind.

49
00:04:25,077 --> 00:04:25,785
Al-Biruni.

50
00:04:25,785 --> 00:04:31,688
In Mains, a frequent question asks how useful foreign accounts are for Indian history.

51
00:04:34,300 --> 00:04:40,446
Give both sides, name whose account is corroborated by what, and always say what the writer came for.

52
00:04:42,267 --> 00:04:45,350
A staff and a journal take their place in the gallery.

53
00:04:45,350 --> 00:04:51,593
Next gallery: a single kind of document, and the biggest argument in the writing of early Indian history.

54
00:04:53,933 --> 00:04:57,762
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

